          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I get migraines about eight times a month. Is that the level where guidance describes prevention, and what lifestyle changes are described?",
          "answer": "Eight migraine days a month is at the edge of the threshold the guidance describes, and the answer depends on how much those attacks disrupt your life.\n\n**When prevention is considered.** The national headache guidance (guideline-migraine-acute-and-preventive-treatment) says preventive treatment is offered when attacks are frequent or disabling enough that acute treatment alone is not enough. It describes the threshold as four or more migraine days a month with some disability. It also says fewer attacks can qualify if they are severe, if acute treatment does not work or cannot be used, or if the person prefers prevention. So at eight days a month, the guidance would point toward discussing prevention if the attacks cause meaningful disability. The stated goal is fewer and milder attacks, not no attacks. A 50 percent drop in migraine days is the usual measure of a good response.\n\n**Lifestyle changes.** The retrieved passages do not describe specific lifestyle changes for migraine. The general clinical reference (condition-migraine) describes what migraine is and what an attack typically involves, but it does not list lifestyle measures. I can't give you a list from memory, so I can't confirm what the guidance recommends here.\n\n**What to discuss with a clinician.** Bring a diary of your attacks: how many days, how long they last, how disabling they are, and what treatments you have tried. Ask whether preventive treatment is worth considering for you and which lifestyle measures are supported for your situation. Also ask about medicine use, since the guidance mentions limits on how often acute medicines should be used.\n\nIf your headaches change in pattern, become sudden or severe, or come with weakness, confusion, or trouble speaking, seek urgent medical care.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
